\section build_cpp_prerequisites 1 Prerequisites

Before proceeding to compile the source code, consult the documents below.

\subsection build_cpp_conventions 1.1 Conventions

See the following document regarding naming conventions in use for the project:
\ref conventions

\subsection build_cpp_boost 1.2 Boost

%QuantLibAddin depends on Boost.  You will need the compiled Boost libraries, the
Boost header files alone are not sufficient.  The build requires Boost 1.58 or
later; this release was tested using Boost 1.83.

\subsection build_cpp_python 1.3 Python 3.x

Some %QuantLibAddin code is autogenerated by gensrc, which is a Python script;
gensrc is included in the source archive.  Before rebuilding %QuantLibAddin you
need to have Python 3.x installed on your machine.  Here is the link to the
Python website: <a href="https://www.python.org/">https://www.python.org/</a>.

For the cmake build, gensrc is invoked automatically when you configure with
<tt>-DRUN_GENSRC=ON</tt>, and cmake locates the Python interpreter for you.

For the Visual Studio build on Windows, if Python is installed on your machine so
that .py files are associated with the Python executable, then the gensrc
scripts should build OK with no modification required.

Otherwise, you might need to edit these files...

\verbatim
    ObjectHandler\gensrc\Makefile.vc
    QuantLibAddin\gensrc\Makefile.vc
\endverbatim

...and set the PYTHON variable equal to the location of the Python executable
on your machine, e.g:

\verbatim
    PYTHON=C:\Program Files\Python312\python.exe
\endverbatim

\section build_cpp_vs 2 Visual Studio

\subsection build_cpp_vs_download 2.1 Download

\subsubsection build_cpp_vs_directories 2.1.1 Directories

The Visual Studio project files for %QuantLibAddin rely on relative pathnames to
refer to the prerequisite projects %QuantLib, gensrc, and ObjectHandler.  In
order for this to work, these projects must reside side by side in the directory
tree, and the name of each project's parent directory must not include the
version number (e.g.  \c %QuantLib not \c QuantLib-1.42.0).  The combined archive
already contains gensrc, ObjectHandler and %QuantLibAddin in the correct layout,
so you only need to add %QuantLib alongside them.

Choose a parent directory on your hard drive under which the build tree will
live.  You can use any path; for purposes of this tutorial the parent directory
is <tt>C:\\</tt>, so the build root becomes <tt>C:\\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt>.
The archive already includes the <tt>QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt> folder, so you
uncompress it <i>into the parent</i> rather than into a folder that is itself
named <tt>QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt>.  After unpacking (below) you should
have the following directory structure:
\verbatim
C:\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\gensrc
C:\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\ObjectHandler
C:\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\QuantLib
C:\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\QuantLibAddin
\endverbatim

\subsubsection build_cpp_vs_uncompress 2.1.2 Uncompress the Source Code

Download the combined source-code archive from:

https://github.com/eehlers/QuantLibAddIn/releases/tag/v1.42.0

The archive already contains a single top-level <tt>QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt>
folder holding gensrc, ObjectHandler and %QuantLibAddin side by side, so
uncompress it into the <i>parent</i> directory (e.g. <tt>C:\\</tt>) to obtain
<tt>C:\\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt>; do not uncompress it into a folder that is
itself named <tt>QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt> or the projects will end up
double-nested.  No separate per-project download of those projects is required.

Download a copy of the source code for the OpenSourceRisk fork of QuantLib.
At the time of this writing, that code has not been packaged for version 1.42.0.
You will have to grab it from their repo:

https://github.com/OpenSourceRisk/QuantLib

Download it alongside the bundled
projects, renaming the target directory to
<tt>C:\\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\\%QuantLib</tt>.

\subsection build_cpp_vs_boostprops 2.2 Configure Boost

The Visual Studio projects locate Boost through two property sheets that are
<b>not</b> part of the source archive (they contain machine-specific paths), so
you must create them by hand before building.

First create <tt>ObjectHandler\\boost.props</tt> - used by ObjectHandler and
%QuantLibAddin - adjusting the paths to your Boost build:
\verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <AdditionalIncludeDirectories>C:\path\to\boost;%(AdditionalIncludeDirectories)</AdditionalIncludeDirectories>
      <LanguageStandard>stdcpp17</LanguageStandard>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(Platform)'=='x64'">
    <Link>
      <AdditionalLibraryDirectories>C:\path\to\boost\stage\lib;%(AdditionalLibraryDirectories)</AdditionalLibraryDirectories>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(Platform)'=='Win32'">
    <Link>
      <AdditionalLibraryDirectories>C:\path\to\boost\stage32\lib;%(AdditionalLibraryDirectories)</AdditionalLibraryDirectories>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
</Project>
\endverbatim

The %QuantLib project uses its own pair of sheets under <tt>%QuantLib\\MSVC</tt>,
which are likewise absent (they are imported unconditionally, so both must
exist).  Create the <tt>%QuantLib\\MSVC</tt> folder and add
<tt>quantlib.x64.user.props</tt> (used for both x64 configurations):
\verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project DefaultTargets="Build" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludePath>C:\path\to\boost;$(IncludePath)</IncludePath>
    <LibraryPath>C:\path\to\boost\stage\lib;$(LibraryPath)</LibraryPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
\endverbatim
and <tt>quantlib.Win32.user.props</tt> (used for both Win32 configurations -
point <tt>LibraryPath</tt> at your 32-bit Boost libraries).  Even if you build
x64 only, create <tt>quantlib.Win32.user.props</tt> too (with any valid
content), otherwise the build stops immediately with
<tt>error MSB4019: The imported project "...\\%QuantLib\\MSVC\\quantlib.x64.user.props" was not found</tt>.

\subsection build_cpp_vs_build 2.3 Build

A single solution file, <tt>%QuantLibAddin\\QuantLibAddin.sln</tt>, is provided.
The platform toolset is selected automatically from the version of Visual Studio
used to open the solution (VS 2022 / v143 and VS 2026 / v145 are supported), so
there is no longer a separate solution file per compiler version.

Open the solution, choose configuration <tt>Release (static runtime)</tt> and
platform <tt>x64</tt>, and execute menu item <tt>Build | Build Solution</tt>.

\subsection build_cpp_vs_test 2.4 Test

Open a DOS prompt in directory
\verbatim
C:\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\QuantLibAddin\Clients\Cpp\bin
\endverbatim

Execute the example program.  The output should appear similar
to the following:
\verbatim
Begin example program.
QuantLibAddin version = 1.42.0
ObjectHandler version = 1.42.0
option PV = 3.84431
End example program.
\endverbatim

\subsection build_cpp_vs_usage 2.5 Usage

\subsubsection build_cpp_vs_include 2.5.1 #include

Add the following directive to your project's source file:
\verbatim
#include <qlo/qladdin.hpp>
\endverbatim

\subsubsection build_cpp_vs_link 2.5.2 Link

Your project's link to the %QuantLibAddin library is established automatically
by the directive
\verbatim
#pragma comment(lib, QLADDIN_LIB_NAME)
\endverbatim
in file <tt>qlo/%auto_link.hpp</tt>

The name of the library depends on your compiler and configuration.
For example, if you're using VS 2026 (v145), platform x64, configuration
Release (static runtime), the library is called
\verbatim
QuantLibAddin\lib\QuantLibObjects-v145-x64-mt-s-1_42_0.lib
\endverbatim

\section build_cpp_cmake 3 CMake

The cmake build runs on both Windows and POSIX systems (e.g. Linux); on Windows
it is x64 only.  It builds each project into its own build tree, so you build the
prerequisites %QuantLib and ObjectHandler first and then point the
%QuantLibAddin build at them.  To see the presets available on your platform, run
<tt>cmake --list-presets</tt>; only presets whose host condition matches your
current operating system are listed.

\subsection build_cpp_cmake_download 3.1 Download

\subsubsection build_cpp_cmake_directories 3.1.1 Directories

This example assumes that you do not have root access to your machine, so that
the entire build environment resides under your home directory.

Let's say your home directory is <tt>/home/username</tt>.  Create a directory
where you will unpack and compile the code.  For purposes of this tutorial it
is:
\verbatim
/home/username/build
\endverbatim

\subsubsection build_cpp_cmake_uncompress 3.1.2 Uncompress the Source Code

Download the combined source-code archive from:

https://github.com/eehlers/QuantLibAddIn/releases/tag/v1.42.0

and uncompress it under <tt>/home/username/build</tt>.  This single archive
contains gensrc, ObjectHandler and %QuantLibAddin, already laid out side by side
under a top-level <tt>%QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt> folder, so unpack it into
<tt>/home/username/build</tt> itself (not into a subfolder already named
<tt>QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt>) to obtain
<tt>/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0</tt>.

Download a copy of the source code for the OpenSourceRisk fork of QuantLib.
At the time of this writing, that code has not been packaged for version 1.42.0.
You will have to grab it from their repo:

https://github.com/OpenSourceRisk/QuantLib

Download it alongside the bundled
projects, renaming the target directory to
<tt>C:\\QuantLibAddin-1.42.0\\%QuantLib</tt>.

After unpacking you will have, among others:
\verbatim
/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/gensrc
/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/ObjectHandler
/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLib
/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLibAddin
\endverbatim

\subsection build_cpp_cmake_build 3.2 Build

The cmake build on Linux is driven by the preset <tt>linux-gcc</tt> (run
<tt>cmake --list-presets</tt> to confirm it is available on your platform).  The
<tt>-DRUN_GENSRC=ON</tt> option runs gensrc (a Python 3 script) to regenerate the
auto-generated source code; it is required for the first build from a fresh
release.

On Windows there is no ready-made preset to select (the <tt>windows-...</tt>
entries in <tt>CMakePresets.json</tt> are hidden base presets, not directly
selectable), so drive the build with an explicit generator instead, for example:
\verbatim
cmake -S . -B build\windows-x64 -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 ^
    -DRUN_GENSRC=ON ^
    -DBoost_USE_STATIC_RUNTIME=ON -DMSVC_LINK_DYNAMIC_RUNTIME=OFF ^
    -DBOOST_INCLUDEDIR=C:\path\to\boost -DBOOST_LIBRARYDIR=C:\path\to\boost\stage\lib ^
    -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="..\QuantLib\build\windows-x64;..\ObjectHandler\build\windows-x64"
cmake --build build\windows-x64 --config Release --target QLADemo
\endverbatim

Build the prerequisites %QuantLib and ObjectHandler first, then configure and
build the %QuantLibAddin wrapper library and the C++ demo client
(<tt>QLADemo</tt>), telling cmake where the prerequisite build trees are.  The
Linux commands are:

\verbatim
cd /home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLibAddin
cmake --preset linux-gcc -DRUN_GENSRC=ON \
    -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="../QuantLib/build/linux-gcc;../ObjectHandler/build/linux-gcc"
cmake --build --preset linux-gcc --target QLADemo
\endverbatim

Building <tt>QLADemo</tt> first builds its prerequisites, the %QuantLibAddin
wrapper library <tt>QuantLibObjects</tt> and the C++ addin library
<tt>AddinCpp</tt>.

\subsection build_cpp_cmake_test 3.3 Test

The demo client is written to the preset's build tree under <tt>cpp/</tt>:

\verbatim
cd /home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLibAddin/build/linux-gcc/cpp
./QLADemo
\endverbatim

This should generate output similar to the following:

\verbatim
Begin example program.
QuantLibAddin version = 1.42.0
ObjectHandler version = 1.42.0
option PV = 3.84431
End example program.
\endverbatim

\subsection build_cpp_cmake_usage 3.4 Usage

To incorporate %QuantLibAddin functionality into your own applications:

Suppose your application consists of source file \c yourapp.cpp:
\verbatim
#include <qlo/qladdin.hpp>
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    std::cout << QuantLibAddin::qlAddinVersion() << std::endl;
}
\endverbatim

To compile your program, ensure that the paths containing the %QuantLibAddin,
ObjectHandler and %QuantLib headers are among those searched by your compiler,
e.g:

\verbatim
g++ -I/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLibAddin \
    -I/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/ObjectHandler \
    -I/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLib \
    -c -o yourapp.o yourapp.cpp
\endverbatim

When linking your program use the -l option to tell gcc to link to the
ObjectHandler, %QuantLib, and %QuantLibAddin libraries, and use -L to specify
the paths containing those libraries, e.g:

\verbatim
g++ -o yourapp yourapp.o \
    -L/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLibAddin/build/linux-gcc \
    -L/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/ObjectHandler/build/linux-gcc \
    -L/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin-1.42.0/QuantLib/build/linux-gcc \
    -lQuantLibObjects -lObjectHandler -lQuantLib
\endverbatim

